branch_response_text:
You are right to question it. I would revise my earlier judgment: **the definition is not fully sufficiently specific for academic writing**, though it is a useful start.

It gives two measurable indicators of “feedback engagement” — comments read and comments incorporated — but it leaves important details unclear, such as:

- what counts as “reading” a comment;
- how reading will be verified or measured;
- what counts as “incorporating” a comment into revision;
- whether partial, indirect, or rejected feedback counts;
- whether engagement includes only quantity, not quality or depth of engagement.

A stronger version might be:

> In this study, feedback engagement refers to students’ behavioral engagement with written feedback, measured by the number of feedback comments they access in the feedback platform and the number of those comments that result in identifiable textual revisions in the subsequent draft.

So, the student’s definition is **operationalized but still underspecified**.

